% \david{Too long and needs updating to match storyline. Too much is spent on structure preservation and too little on our main contribution.}
% Distribution matching (DM) is a versatile domain-invariant representation learning technique that has been applied to tasks such as fair classification, domain adaptation, and domain translation. A key challenge in DM is ensuring that latent representations remain meaningful and robust. Preserving the inherent geometry of the data has been shown to disentangle the representation space, enabling the discovery of better latent structures. To address this, prior works have introduced structure-preserving regularization inspired by the Gromov-Wasserstein distance, which explicitly retains geometric relationships in the latent space. Variational methods such as variational autoencoders (VAEs) have since incorporated this regularization, allowing comparisons between latent and observed spaces even when their dimensions differ. However, these methods often rely on simplistic priors, such as isotropic Gaussians, which constrain the latent space and distort meaningful structure.

% We propose a new approach that replaces these restrictive priors with expressive, learnable score-based priors. These priors naturally integrate with structure-preserving regularization and are better suited to capturing complex data distributions. A major challenge with score-based models, however, is their computational cost when calculating likelihoods. Our insight is that likelihood computation is unnecessary for encoder updates. We prove that the required gradients can be computed using only a single evaluation of the score function, significantly improving computational efficiency.

% Our framework further incorporates semantically meaningful structure from latent spaces in pretrained foundation models. This ensures that the learned representations not only preserve geometric consistency but also align with semantic information relevant to downstream tasks. Through extensive experiments, we demonstrate that our approach outperforms existing methods, achieving superior latent representations for tasks such as fair classification, domain adaptation, and domain translation

Distribution matching (DM) is a versatile domain-invariant representation learning technique that has been applied to tasks such as fair classification, domain adaptation, and domain translation. 
Non-parametric DM methods struggle with scalability and adversarial DM approaches suffer from instability and mode collapse.
While likelihood-based methods are a promising alternative, they often impose unnecessary biases through fixed priors or require explicit density models (e.g., flows) that can be challenging to train.
We address this limitation by introducing a novel approach to training likelihood-based DM using expressive score-based prior distributions.
Our key insight is that gradient-based DM training only requires the prior's score function---not its density---allowing us to train the prior via denoising score matching. 
This approach eliminates biases from fixed priors (e.g., in VAEs), enabling more effective use of geometry-preserving regularization, while avoiding the challenge of learning an explicit prior density model (e.g., a flow-based prior). Our method also demonstrates better stability and computational efficiency compared to other diffusion-based priors (e.g., LSGM). 
Furthermore, experiments demonstrate superior performance across multiple tasks, establishing our score-based method as a stable and effective approach to distribution matching. Source code available at \url{https://github.com/inouye-lab/SAUB}.


\end{abstract}

