\section{Computational Results}


\subsection{Algorithm for Deep Weighted Polynomial Approximation}\label{subsec:deep-weighted-poly}

We seek to approximate a real-valued function \(f(x)\) by a \emph{deep weighted polynomial} of the form
\[
Q(x) \;=\; w(x)^\gamma\,h^{(L)}(x),
\]
where the weight \(w(x)\colon[a,b] \to \mathbb{R}_{>0}\) is typically chosen to enforce exponential decay or localization, and \(\gamma \ge 0\) is a tunable exponent. The function \(h^{(L)}(x)\) is the output of a scalar-valued \emph{computational graph} of depth \(L\), where each layer is constructed recursively via the polynomial update
\[
h^{(\ell)}(x) \;=\; \sum_{i=0}^{w_\ell - 1} a^{(\ell)}_i \left(h^{(\ell-1)}(x)\right)^i, \quad h^{(0)}(x) = x,
\]
and the parameter vector \(\theta = (a^{(1)}, a^{(2)}, \dots, a^{(L)}) \in \mathbb{R}^{n_{\mathrm{deep}}}\) comprises all coefficients. This approach generalizes the framework introduced by Jarlebring et al.~\cite{Jar}, where the computational graph formalism is used to represent and differentiate matrix functions. Here, we specialize it to nonlinear polynomial approximants over one-dimensional input, optimizing over the coefficient vector \(\theta\) to minimize the weighted least-squares loss
\[
L(\theta) \;=\; \sum_{i=1}^N \left(Q(x_i; \theta) - f(x_i)\right)^2 \Delta x,
\]
where \(\{x_i\}_{i=1}^N \subset [a,b]\) is a uniform sampling grid and \(\Delta x = (b - a)/N\) is the quadrature weight. The loss is minimized using gradient-based optimization, and gradients \(\nabla_\theta L\) are computed efficiently by automatic differentiation through the computational graph.

\smallskip

Algorithm~\ref{alg:deep-weighted-general} summarizes the overall procedure. A scalar DAG is constructed with input node \(x = h^{(0)}\), followed by a sequence of composition layers whose widths \(\{w_\ell\}\) and total parameter count \(n_{\rm deep} = \sum_\ell w_\ell\) are specified in advance. The graph consists only of multiplication and linear-combination operations, making it amenable to efficient symbolic or automatic differentiation. To avoid local minima, we perform \(R\) random restarts with Gaussian initialization of the coefficients. For each restart, we iteratively minimize the loss \(L(\theta)\) by backpropagating the residuals through the graph \cite{Jar} and updating the parameter vector. The best set of coefficients \(\theta^*\) is retained. The weight \(w(x)^\gamma\) introduces asymmetry into the approximation, focusing accuracy in regions where \(w(x)\) is large—typically those with challenging function behavior—without increasing global polynomial complexity.


\begin{algorithm}[h]
\caption{Deep Weighted Polynomial via Computational Graphs}
\label{alg:deep-weighted-general}
\smallskip
\textbf{Input:} \(f\colon[a,b]\to\mathbb R\), weight \(w\colon[a,b]\to\mathbb R_{>0}\), exponent \(\gamma\ge0\), samples \(\{x_i\}_{i=1}^N\), layers \(L\), widths \(\{w_\ell\}\), restarts \(R\), step~\(\eta\).  
Let \(n_{\rm deep}=\sum_{\ell=1}^L w_\ell\).  

\textbf{Output:} \(\theta^*=(a^{(1)},\dots,a^{(L)})\in\mathbb R^{n_{\rm deep}}\).

\begin{enumerate}[itemsep=0pt]
\item \emph{Graph construction:}  
  Create DAG \(\mathcal G\) with input \(h^{(0)}=x\); for \(\ell=1,\dots,L\), nodes \((h^{(\ell-1)})^i\), \(i=0,\dots,w_\ell-1\), and one linear-combination node  
  \[
    h^{(\ell)}=\sum_{i=0}^{w_\ell-1}a^{(\ell)}_i\bigl(h^{(\ell-1)}\bigr)^i.
  \]
  Final output node computes \(Q(x)=w(x)^\gamma\,h^{(L)}(x)\).

\item Set \(L^*\gets+\infty\).

\item \textbf{for} \(r=1\) to \(R\) \textbf{do}
  \begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*), itemsep=0pt]
    \item Initialize \(\theta\sim\mathcal N(0,I)\).
    \item \textbf{Repeat until convergence:}
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*), itemsep=0pt]
      \item Compute loss  
      \[
      L(\theta)=\sum_i\left[Q(x_i;\theta)-f(x_i)\right]^2\,\Delta x.
      \]
      \item Compute \(\nabla_\theta L\) by backpropagation through \(\mathcal G\) \cite{Jar}.
      \item Update parameters: \(\theta\gets\theta-\eta\nabla_\theta L\).
    \end{enumerate}
    \item If \(L(\theta)<L^*\), update \(\theta^*\gets\theta\), \(L^*\gets L(\theta)\).
  \end{enumerate}

\item \textbf{return} \(\theta^*\).
\end{enumerate}
\end{algorithm}



\medskip

\noindent\textbf{Implementation details.}  In our experiments we take \(N\approx600\) uniformly 
spaced sample points on \([a,b]\), set \(\gamma=1\) for the weighted variant and \(\gamma=0\) for
the unweighted baseline, and choose \(R=5\) random restarts.  Convergence is declared when the
relative change in \(L\) drops below \(10^{-12}\).  This framework readily extends to other weight
functions and to different loss functions (e.g.\ \(L_\infty\) or relative error), and forms the basis
for our deep weighted polynomial approximations throughout the paper.



\subsection{Exponential Function}

Polynomials fail to approximate functions on unbounded domains because any non-constant polynomial \( p(x) \) diverges to \(\pm\infty\) as \(x \to \pm\infty\). For example, only the constant polynomial can approximate \(e^x\) with finite error on \((-\infty, 0]\); therefore, the minimax error is no less than \(1/2\), since \(e^0 = 1\) while \(e^{-\infty} = 0\). This challenge highlights the limitations of classical approximation theory in settings where the target function exhibits extreme or asymmetric behavior at infinity.

As a first example, we consider a function that exhibits two distinct behaviors: it diverges exponentially as \(x \to -\infty\), and decays rapidly as \(x \to +\infty\). Such discontinuous structure, particularly in the derivatives, is well-suited to deep polynomial approximants.

In our approach, the weight function is defined asymmetrically as
\[
w(x) =
\begin{cases}
1, & x < 0, \\[1mm]
e^{-x^2}, & x \ge 0,
\end{cases}
\]
so that the overall approximant takes the form
\[
Q(x) = w(x) \cdot q\bigl(p(x)\bigr).
\]
Although \(w(x)\) is continuous, and its first derivative satisfies \(w'(0^-) = w'(0^+) = 0\), the second derivative is discontinuous at \(x=0\). Explicitly, we have
\[
w''(x) =
\begin{cases}
0, & x < 0, \\
(4x^2 - 2) e^{-x^2}, & x \ge 0,
\end{cases}
\]
so that \(w''(0^+) = -2\). This creates a challenge for smooth approximation, especially near \(x=0\), which requires the composition \(q(p(x))\) to adapt across a non-smooth interface.

To ensure that the deep polynomial \(q(p(x))\) accurately tracks \(f(x) = e^{-x}\) across both regions, it must satisfy
\[
Q(x) =
\begin{cases}
q(p(x)) \approx e^{-x}, & x < 0, \\[1mm]
e^{-x^2} \cdot q(p(x)) \approx e^{-x}, & x \ge 0,
\end{cases}
\]
which is equivalent to enforcing
\[
q(p(x)) \approx \frac{e^{-x}}{e^{-x^2}} = e^{-x + x^2}
\qquad \text{for } x \ge 0.
\]
Thus, the same deep polynomial \(q(p(x))\) must serve the dual role of approximating \(e^{-x}\) on the left and \(e^{-x+x^2}\) on the right. This type of behavior cannot be captured by a single unweighted polynomial of modest degree, given minmax approximation relies on equioscillation, but arises naturally from the compositional structure of our deep graph-based approximation. The ability to resolve such non-smoothness is a key strength of the deep polynomial approach \cite{KY}. 

