\subsection{ Generalized symmetric Freud weight on $\mathbb R$.} \label{sec:generalizedweight}

Consider the following generalization of the symmetric weight $w_{\lambda}(x):=\exp(-|x|^{\lambda})$ for $\lambda>1$.
Let $w=\exp(-Q(x)): \mathbb R\to \mathbb R$ where the external field $Q$ is even and continuous and satisfies the following conditions: 
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] $Q'$ exists and is positive in $(0,\infty).$
\item[(b)] $xQ'(x)$ is strictly increasing in $(0,\infty)$
with $\lim_{|x|\to 0^+}xQ'(x)=0.$
\item[(c)] The function $\frac{xQ'(x)}{Q(x)} \sim 1$ uniformly 
for large enough argument.
\end{itemize}
Then 
\[
\frac{2}{\pi}\int_{0}^{1}\frac{atQ'(at)}{\sqrt{1-t^2}}dt=n,\, a>0
\]
has a unique solution $a=a_n$ and for every polynomial $p_n$ with degree at most $n$, $n\geq 1$ and 
\[
||p_nw||_{\mathbb R}=||p_nw||_{[-a,a]}
\]

Building on the classical Freud weight $w_\lambda(x)=\exp(-|x|^\lambda)$, we extend this notion by optimizing over the two‐parameter family
\[
  Q(x) = c\,|x|^n,\qquad c>0,\;n>1,
\]
so that $w(x)=\exp(-Q(x))$.  One checks that:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*, label=(\alph*)]
  \item $Q'(x)=cn\,x^{\,n-1}>0$ for $x>0$;
  \item $xQ'(x)=cn\,x^n$ is strictly increasing on $(0,\infty)$ with $\lim_{x\to0^+}xQ'(x)=0$;
  \item \(\displaystyle\frac{xQ'(x)}{Q(x)}=\frac{cn\,x^n}{c\,x^n}=n\), hence asymptotically constant.
\end{enumerate}
We then fit \((c,n)\) by minimizing the discrete \(L_2\)-error of a deep weighted polynomial approximant to \(e^{-x}\) on \([-3,20]\). Figure~\ref{fig:opt_vs_baseline} compares the resulting optimized field to the baseline Gaussian choice \((c,n)=(1,2)\). Even with a simpler choice like \((c,n)=(1,1)\), the approximation remains nontrivial—since the weight only acts on \(x>0\), the growth for \(x<0\) must still be matched by the deep polynomial, which has exponential degree with respect to the number of layers. While we use \(e^{-x}\) as a controlled test case, in practice the growth and decay behavior may be unknown, as in the Airy function. Our goal is to approximate such functions using combinations of elementary functions.


