The validation of VaN3Twin was conducted at two distinct levels. At the propagation level, the RSSI values measured by the real $R_x$ vehicle were used as a baseline for comparison against those virtually replicated. At the applicative level, alignment in packet reception between the virtual $R_x$ and the physical dataset was evaluated. For this second analysis, an ad-hoc metric---referred to as the \gls{dr}---was introduced to assess the extent to which VaN3Twin enhances overall replication accuracy. The \gls{dr} is a punctual, fine-grained metric designed to quantify the level of disagreement between VaN3Twin and a baseline in terms of decision outcomes. Specifically, let $\mathcal{M}$ and $\mathcal{R}$ represent the sets of time instants at which reception decisions are made by the baseline and VaN3Twin, respectively. The symmetric difference $\mathcal{M} \Delta \mathcal{R}$ thus captures all instances in which the two disagree on whether a packet was successfully received. The \gls{dr} is defined as follows:
\begin{equation}
    DR = \left|\mathcal{M}\Delta\mathcal{R}\right|/\left|\mathcal{M}\cup \mathcal{R}\right|
\end{equation}
%\gls{dr}, as presented in Section~\ref{sec:physical-results-coexistence}. 

%To assess how well VaN3Twin increases the overall reliability when compared to the baseline model, a fine-grained metric is needed to quantify their level of disagreement upon a certain decision. Let $\mathcal{M}$ and $\mathcal{R}$ denote the sets of time instants where a positive decision is made based on the outputs of the baseline model and VaN3Twin, respectively. The symmetric difference $\mathcal{M} \Delta \mathcal{R}$ thus captures all cases in which the two models disagree on the reception outcome. The definition of the \gls{dr} is then:
%\begin{equation}
%    DR = \left|\mathcal{M}\Delta\mathcal{R}\right|/\left|\mathcal{M}\cup \mathcal{R}\right|
%\end{equation}

To enable a comprehensive evaluation of the differences between stochastic models---as adopted by current V2X simulation tools---a ray-based propagation model, and real-world measurements, the exact same simulation was also conducted using the vanilla version of ms-van3t. 
In this configuration, ms-van3t relies on a stochastic propagation model based on the 3GPP TR 36.885~\cite{3gpp_tr_36_885} specifications, which incorporates distance-dependent, probabilistic \gls{los} status determination. More specifically, the \gls{los} probability is:
\begin{align} \label{eq:los-probability}
P_{LoS} = \min\left(\frac{18}{d}, 1\right)\left(1 - e^{-d/36}\right) + e^{-d/36},
\end{align}
where $d$ is the distance between the transmitter and the receiver. Notably, this model is well aligned with the conditions of both scenarios. The rural campaign featured a full \gls{los} between transmitter and receiver, closely matching theoretical assumptions. Meanwhile, in the urban scenario, the transmitter was placed at a height comparable to that of a typical cellular microcell---precisely the conditions for which the model was designed to operate. These represent ideal conditions for the model’s intended use, making the comparison particularly relevant. In conclusion, both the real-vehicle field tests and the simulations with VaN3Twin and ms-van3t used the IEEE 802.11p communication stack.

