\section{VaN3Twin for multi-rat coexistence} \label{sec:coexistence-main-section}

This section presents the underlying logic of the VaN3Twin module designed to manage multi-\gls{rat} coexistence, taking into account partial or full spectrum overlap between concurrent transmissions across different vehicular technologies.

%This section introduces the logic behind \texttt{TxTracker}, the module for ms-van3t and VaN3Twin designed to handle multi-\gls{rat} coexistence and account for partial or complete spectrum overlap.

\subsection{Proposed coexistence implementation} 
\label{sec:coexistence}

The vanilla version of ms-van3t includes different default models for each \gls{rat} to simulate the signal propagation.
%Specifically, a log-normal model is used for IEEE 802.11p, a model based on 3GPP TR~36.885~\cite{3gpp_tr_36_885} specifications is used for LTE-V2X, and for NR-V2X a model following 3GPP TR~38.901~\cite{3gpp_tr_38_901} was implemented. In 3GPP-based models, a probabilistic \gls{los} determination mechanism is also implemented. 
However, the LTE-V2X and NR-V2X modules typically rely on 3GPP-based propagation models \cite{3gpp_tr_36_885, 3gpp_tr_38_901} that are incompatible with those used by the IEEE 802.11p module, and vice versa. Furthermore, as previously discussed, the default channel models in ms-van3t employ probabilistic \gls{los} determination, limiting their accuracy in complex scenarios. To partially mitigate these limitations, ms-van3t allows manual adjustment of the channel condition through external inputs. This can be achieved either by directly injecting the \gls{los} status---e.g., from a ray tracing engine---or by generating a 2D building map within the simulator for automatic \gls{los}/\gls{nlos} classification. However, the latter approach is both time-consuming and imprecise, as it represents buildings using basic box geometries. In contrast, VaN3Twin leverages ray tracing to deliver significantly improved modeling accuracy and a unified channel abstraction that is consistent across different \glspl{rat}. This unified approach enables consistent and realistic channel estimation, as previously formalized in equation~\eqref{eq:channel_matrix_time}. Such consistency is especially critical for multi-\gls{rat} coexistence scenarios, where realistic modeling of shared or partially overlapping spectrum is required.

%While ns-3 already includes a module for simulating IEEE 802.11p (DSRC), ms-van3t and VaN3Twin extend it by integrating two C-V2X modules. The first enables the simulation of LTE-V2X, a communication technology derived from LTE and standardized in 3GPP Release 14. The second models NR-V2X, a \gls{rat} based on NR and defined in 3GPP Release 16~\cite{3GPPNR}. To incorporate both C-V2X modules, the 5G-LENA project is implemented, developed by the Centre Tecnològic de Telecomunicacions de Catalunya (CTTC)~\cite{5g-lena} and the LTE-V2X module from TU Dortmund University~\cite{eckermann-lte-v2x-model}. 

%However, in ms-van3t each module has its own parameters and procedures to ensure standard-compliant communication between simulated road users. 
%Notably, modules for 3GPP \glspl{rat} typically rely on propagation models that are incompatible with those used by the DSRC module, and vice versa. More specifically, in ns-3, DSRC relies on a channel model based on the 3GPP TR 36.885~\cite{3gpp_tr_36_885} specifications, while NR-V2X on 3GPP TR 38.901~\cite{3gpp_tr_38_901}. These models incorporate probabilistic \gls{los} determination and simplified path loss formulas. To improve the accuracy of the 3GPP-based propagation model for NR-V2X in ms-van3t, the channel condition can also be manually adjusted based on external input. This is supported either by directly injecting the \gls{los} status or by generating a 2D building map within ns-3 for automatic determination. However, the latter is both time-consuming and limited in accuracy, as buildings are represented using simplistic box geometries. 
%Conversely, the VaN3Twin use of ray tracing offers the dual advantage of increased modeling accuracy and a unified channel model suitable for different \glspl{rat}, providing a consistent method for calculating the channel~\eqref{eq:channel_matrix_time}. This feature is particularly crucial when simulating the coexistence of multiple \glspl{rat}, enabling realistic modeling of shared spectrum with partial or full overlap. 

In a V2X simulation scenario, let $\mathcal{W}$ denote the set of simulated \glspl{rat} for which the coexistence module is enabled. Each $w \in \mathcal{W}$ operates over a frequency band of bandwidth $B^{(w)}$, centered at carrier frequency $f_c^{(w)}$, and utilizes an \gls{ofdm} waveform with $L^{(w)}$ subcarriers spaced by $\Delta f^{(w)}$. The corresponding sub-channel grid is given by:
%When simulating a V2X communication, let $\mathcal{W}$ be the set of simulated \glspl{rat} with the coexistence module enabled. Each $w \in \mathcal{W}$ occupies a spectrum band with bandwidth $B^{(w)}$ centered at $f_c^{(w)}$, and employs an \gls{ofdm} waveform with $L^{(w)}$ subcarriers spaced by $\Delta f^{(w)}$. The frequency-domain subcarrier grid is:
\begin{multline}
    \mathcal{F}^{(w)} = \left\{ 
    f_c^{(w)} + l \cdot \Delta f^{(w)} 
    \,\middle|\,
    l \in \mathbb{Z}, ~
    f_c^{(w)} + l \cdot \Delta f^{(w)} \in \right.\\
    \left. \left[ f_c^{(w)} - \frac{B^{(w)}}{2},~f_c^{(w)} + \frac{B^{(w)}}{2} \right]
    \right\}, ~\text{with} ~ l \in L^{(w)}.
\end{multline}

To evaluate inter-\gls{rat} interference, a global resource grid must be defined that encompasses all technologies in the set $\mathcal{W}$. For each $w \in \mathcal{W}$, the corresponding grid is defined as:
%To evaluate inter-\gls{rat} interference, a global time-frequency resource grid must be defined, incorporating all technologies. For each $w \in \mathcal{W}$, its resource grid is:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{Z}^{(w)} = \mathcal{F}^{(w)} \times \mathcal{S}^{(w)},
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{S}^{(w)}$ is the set of corresponding time slots.
The overall resource grid is defined as:
\begin{align}
    \mathcal{Z} = \mathcal{F} \times \mathcal{S}, \quad
    \text{with } 
    \mathcal{F} = \bigcup_{w \in \mathcal{W}} \mathcal{F}^{(w)},\quad
    \mathcal{S} = \bigcup_{w \in \mathcal{W}} \mathcal{S}^{(w)}. 
\end{align}

Given a resource block $z \in \mathcal{Z}$, let $\mathcal{W}(z) \subseteq \mathcal{W}$ denote the set of \glspl{rat} actively using it. To monitor potential inter-\gls{rat} interference, the coexistence module continuously observes the communication channel and tracks all transmissions from each $w \in \mathcal{W}(z)$.\footnote{This feature enables concurrent simulation of IEEE 802.11p and NR-V2X.} To support this functionality, a set of tracking objects $\mathcal{H}$ is introduced. For each transmitting vehicle $v_n \in \mathcal{V}_{\text{tx}}(t) \subseteq \mathcal{V}_{\text{active}}(t)$ at time $t$, a corresponding tracking object $H_{v_n} \in \mathcal{H}$ is defined as:
\begin{align}
    H_{v_n} = \left( w \in \mathcal{W},~f_c^{(w)},~B^{(w)},~T,~S(f) \right),
\end{align}
where $T$ is the transmission duration and $S(f)$ the Power Spectral Density (PSD).

Let us now consider two vehicles transmitting using technologies $w$ and $w'$, both belonging to $\mathcal{W}(z)$, with $w \neq w'$. The SINR on resource block $z$ for the transmission using technology $w$ is computed as:
\begin{align}\label{eq:sinr-resblock}
    \text{SINR}_{w,z} = \frac{P_{\text{Rx}, w, z}}{N_{0,z} + \sum_{w' \neq w} P_{\text{Rx}, w', z}},
\end{align}
where $P_{\text{Rx}, w, z}$ denotes the received power of the desired signal, $P_{\text{Rx}, w', z}$ represents the interfering power from technology $w'$, and $N_{0,z}$ is the noise power on resource block $z$. The contribution of $P_{\text{Rx}, w', z}$ in equation~\eqref{eq:sinr-resblock} is computed as follows. Let $v_{\text{int}}$ be an interfering vehicle using technology $w' \in \mathcal{W}(z)$, with an associated tracking object $H_{v_{\text{int}}}$. To discard negligible interference, a threshold on the total path gain $\mathcal{G}_{\text{thr}}$ is introduced. For a receiving vehicle $v_r$ using technology $w \neq w'$, interference from $v_{\text{int}}$ is ignored if the total path gain $\mathcal{G}_{v_{\text{int}}, v_r}$ is less than the minimum threshold, i.e., $\mathcal{G}_{v_{\text{int}}, v_r} < \mathcal{G}_{\text{thr}}$. Otherwise, the received interference power is calculated based on the PSD $S$ and the total path gain $\mathcal{G}_{v_{\text{int}}, v_r}$.

The overall SINR for technology $w$ is obtained by computing a bandwidth-weighted average over all resource blocks $z \in \mathcal{Z}$:
\begin{align}
    \text{SINR}_{w} = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{Z}|} \sum_{z \in \mathcal{Z}} \omega_z \cdot \text{SINR}_{w,z},
\end{align}
where $\omega_z$ represents the bandwidth weight associated with block $z$.

\begin{algorithm} [!t]
\caption{Inter-RAT SINR Evaluation}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\REQUIRE Set of active \glspl{rat} $w \in \mathcal{W}$
\REQUIRE Sub channels $\mathcal{F}^{(w)} ~ \forall ~ w \in \mathcal{W}$ 
\REQUIRE Time slots $\mathcal{S}^{(w)} ~ \forall ~w \in \mathcal{W}$

\FORALL{$w \in \mathcal{W}$}
    \STATE Set $\mathcal{F} \gets \mathcal{F} \cup \mathcal{F}^{(w)}$
    \STATE Set $\mathcal{S} \gets \mathcal{S} \cup \mathcal{S}^{(w)}$
\ENDFOR

\STATE $\mathcal{Z} \gets \mathcal{F} \times\mathcal{S} $
\STATE Track each $v_n \in \mathcal{V}_{\text{tx}}(t) \subseteq \mathcal{V}_\text{active}(t)$

\FORALL{blocks $z \in \mathcal{Z}$}
    \STATE Identify set of active \glspl{rat} $\mathcal{W}(z) \subseteq \mathcal{W}$

    \FORALL{RATs $w \in \mathcal{W}(z)$}
        \STATE Initialize interferers $v_\text{int}$ contribution
        \FORALL{$w' \in \mathcal{W}(z),~w' \neq w$}
            \FORALL{tracked interferers $v_\text{int}$ using $w'$}
                \IF{total path gain $\mathcal{G}_{v_{\text{int}}, v_r} \geq \mathcal{G}_{\text{thr}}$}
                    \STATE $P_\text{int} \gets P_\text{int} + P_{\text{Rx},w',h}$
                \ENDIF
            \ENDFOR
        \ENDFOR
        \STATE Compute $\text{SINR}_{w,z}$
    \ENDFOR
\ENDFOR

\FORALL{$w \in \mathcal{W}$}
    \STATE Compute average $\text{SINR}_{w}$ weighted by $\omega_z$
\ENDFOR

\end{algorithmic}
\label{algo:sinr}
\end{algorithm}

For clarity and ease of understanding, the entire SINR computation process described above is also summarized in Algorithm~\ref{algo:sinr}. Furthermore, to illustrate the capabilities of the coexistence module, a simulation of a vehicular scenario with multi-\glspl{rat} communication is presented and analyzed in the following subsection. It is important to emphasize that the objective is not to perform an exhaustive co-channel interference study for vehicular multi-\gls{rat} simulations, but rather to assess the differences in \gls{sinr} calculation and its applicative level impact when interference from a coexisting technology is modeled using VaN3Twin, compared to ms-van3t. This comparison serves to quantify the benefits of integrating such a module, along with accurate physical-layer modeling, within a \gls{ndt} framework.

