    \label{fig:nlosv_pdf}
\end{figure}
This limitation is evident in Figure~\ref{fig:nlosv_pdf}, which presents the SINR \gls{pdf} for a subset of vehicles frequently experiencing \gls{nlosv} conditions during the simulation. Here, the distribution obtained with VaN3Twin retains the previously observed Gaussian-like profile. Conversely, the two versions of ms-van3t notably overestimate SINR---irrespective of whether \gls{nlosv} conditions are treated as \gls{nlos}---both showing a pronounced peak around 10~dB.
%Unlike the general scenario, the resulting distribution is quasi-unimodal, which can be attributed to the fact that the selected vehicles remain in \gls{los} for the majority of the simulation, and the propagation model only switches states in the presence of transient obstructions caused by nearby vehicles.
In conclusion, the Gaussian-like distributions observed in the VaN3Twin results of Figures~\ref{fig:generic_pdf} and \ref{fig:nlosv_pdf} reflect a more realistic propagation behavior, as discussed in Section~\ref{sec:validation}.

