% \subsection{Scenario template, environment, and user stories} \label{sec:design:composites}

% \noindent
\paragraph{Scenario template, environment, and user stories}
Our \mm{} includes two composites to represent concrete BDD scenarios, namely \keyword{ScenarioTemplate} and \keyword{ScenarioVariant}.
% The template-variant separation is inspired by~\cite{Alferez2019}, where BDD templates are designed for common data operations in the financial domain.
% A \texttt{Scenario Outline} (see \cref{lst:example-gherkin}) in Gherkin serves a role similar to a template, whose variants are rows in a table under the \texttt{Examples} keyword.
A template integrates a \keyword{Scenario} with a scene and fluent clauses, while a variant combines a template with task variations and reified relations linking to expected scene elements, along with additional clauses needed to evaluate the concrete scenario.
% \cref{fig:bdd-pick-variant} shows an example of this structure realized for the picking scenario.
Following the original BDD formulation~\cite{north2006bdd}, a \keyword{UserStory} (equivalent to \texttt{Feature} in Gherkin) is a composite of scenario variants.

