\noindent
{\bf{Open Problem.}}~ {\it Give the optimal upper bound on the length of the 
shortest solution sequence to the pebble motion problem on $N$-vertex trees, 
with respect to $N$.}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

Kornhauser, Miller, and Spirakis \cite{715921} state that the upper bound for the above problem
belongs to $\Ord(N^2)$, but their proof is incorrect. In fact, their method only establishes
an $\Ord(N^3)$ bound (see Remark\tume\ref{counterexample} in the Appendix).
Since then, including the authors of the original paper\cite{715921}, no one has 
clarified the truth of this statement. Here we note that, in the worst case, it may take 
$\Omega(Nn)$ steps to move $n$ pebbles from one end to another of an $N$-vertex 
board tree. Additionally, in the worst case, the parameter $n$ may be in $\Omega(N)$. 
Therefore, their estimate $\Ord(N^2)$ in \cite{715921} can be replaced with 
$\Theta(N^2)$.
For over 40 years, this problem has remained open. 
In fact, attempts to genuinely lower the upper bound of $\Ord(N^3)$ proven in~\cite{715921} 
have been made up to the present day\cite{WOS:001177663400002, WOS:000077614700003}. 
However, to the author's knowledge, the current best result, as shown in the 2024 
paper\cite{WOS:001177663400002}, still does not fall below the $\Ord(N^3)$ threshold.
