In this paper, we show that the problem mentioned above has a solution sequence of 
moves of length \textrm{\~{O}}$(N^2)$. 
A more precise description of the results obtained in this paper follows.
%さて、この論文では、上記の問題の計算量評価が\textrm{\~{O}}$(N^2)$に属することを示す。
%本論文で得られた結果のより正確な記述は以下の通りである。

A path $I=v_1 \cdots v_d$ of a connected graph $G$ is called an \textit{isthmus} if 
(1) every edge of $I$ is a bridge of $G$, 
(2) every vertex of $I$ is a cutvertex of $G$, and
(3) ${\rm deg}_G (v_i)=2$ for $1 < i < d$.
An isthmus with $d$ vertices is called a $d$-isthmus.

First, let us clarify one point. 
In the pebble motion problem on a general tree $G$, any two configurations of pebbles on $G$ are not guaranteed to be rearrangeable through a sequence of moves. 
Therefore, we will only consider {\it feasible} puzzles, that is, the puzzles in which, given any two configurations $f$ and $g$, a sequence of moves exists to rearrange from $f$ to $g$.
According to Theorem A discussed later, a given pair $(G, P)$ is a feasible pebble motion puzzle if and only if the pair satisfies the inequality $n+k < N$, where $n$ is the number $|P|$ of pebbles on $G$, $k$ is the maximum size of isthmuses in $G$, and $N$ is the order of $G$. 
Thus, we will restrict the instances of the following combinatorial problem, the PEBBLE MOTION PROBLEM ON TREES, to puzzles that satisfy such conditions.
One more thing to clarify is that no pebble can move across an isthmus with a size greater than or equal to the number of empty spaces $N - n$. 
Therefore, in cases with such isthmuses, it is sufficient to handle the reconfiguration only on the individual 'feasible' connected components that are divided by these isthmuses. 
%ここで一つ断っておかなければならないことがある。
%一般の木$G$の上のpebble motion problemにおいて、その上の任意の二つのpebblesの配置がmoveの系列で互いに移りあうとは限らない、ということである。
%したがって、ここでは、任意の二つの配置$f,g$が与えられたとき、必ず$f$から$g$を得るmoveの系列が存在するような（つまり、feasibleな）パズルのみを考える。
%後述する定理Aによれば、与えられたペア$(G,P)$上のpebble motion puzzleがfeasibleであるための条件は、$G$の最大isthmus長を$k$、$G$の頂点サイズを$N$、
%$G$上のpebble達の個数$|P|$を$n$として、$n+k < N$という関係式が成り立つこと、と同値である。
%したがって、我々は、下記の組合せ最適化問題のインスタンスを、このような条件を満たすパズルに制限する。

\begin{prob}[PEBBLE MOTION PROBLEM ON TREES]\label{PMPonTree} \hfill
\begin{namelist}{INSTANCE}
\item[{\it{Instance}}] \rm A tree $G$ with $N$ vertices, 
the size $k$ of a longest isthmus of $G$, 
the set of pebbles $P:=\{1,\ldots,n\}$ such that 
$n+k < N$ holds, 
the initial configuration $f$ and the goal configuration $g$ on the pair $(G,P)$.
\item[{\it{Task}}] \rm Output an explicit sequence of moves to rearrange from the initial configuration $f$ to the goal configuration $g$. 
\end{namelist}
\end{prob}

The following is our main theorem. 
\begin{thm}\label{main}
The PEBBLE MOTION PROBLEM ON TREES can be solved with a time complexity of $\Ord(Nn+n^2\log(\min\{n,k\}))$.
\end{thm}

Let $L_{\min}(G,P,f,g)$ denote the minimum number of moves to rearrange from a configuration $f$ to another configuration $g$ on the pair $(G,P)$. 
The time complexity of the PEBBLE MOTION PROBLEM ON TREES is clearly in $\Omega(L_{\min}(G,P,f,g))$. Hence we have:

\begin{cor}
The length of the shortest solution sequence of the PEBBLE MOTION PROBLEM ON TREES is in $\Ord(Nn + n^2 \log(\min\{n,k\}))$.
\end{cor}

The above result is somewhat surprising.
In fact, when the size $k$ of a longest isthmus is in $\Ord(2^{N/n})$, 
the length of the shortest solution sequence belongs to $\Ord(Nn)$, 
and the estimate $\Ord(N^2)$ in\cite{715921} is established in a stronger form.
We believe that the conjecture below will also hold.
%上記の結果は、いささか驚くべきものである。実際、最大isthmus長$k$が$\Ord(1)$となる場合には、 
%the length of the shortest solution sequenceが$\Ord(Nn)$に属しており、
%Conjecture\tume\ref{FOCS84}の言明は、より強い形で成立している。
%ただし、我々はConjecture\tume\ref{715921}自体は残念ながらおそらく間違っており、
%実際には以下に示す予想が成立するであろうと信じている。

\begin{conj}
The length of the shortest solution sequence of the PEBBLE MOTION PROBLEM ON TREES is in ${\mathrm{\Theta}}(Nn + n^2 \log(\min\{n,k\}))$.
\end{conj}

As a matter of fact, considering the combinatorial structure of the base cases described in Section\tume\ref{base-cases}, 
it would be extremely difficult to further improve on the order of length of a minimum solution sequence we have given in this paper.
%実際、Section\tume\ref{base-cases}で述べるbase casesの組合せ構造を勘案するとき、
%我々が本論文で与えた計算量評価を更に改善することは極めて困難であろうと推測される。

We will refer to the problem of changing the input graph in the PEBBLE MOTION PROBLEM ON TREES 
to a general connected $N$-vertex graph as the PEBBLE MOTION PROBLEM ON GENERAL GRAPHS. 
Note that the input pair $(G, P)$ of the problem is again restricted to be a feasible pair, that is, we assume 
that the input board graph $G$ is not a cycle, that $N-n$ is greater than or equal to $2$, and that the number 
of vertices of any isthmus in $G$ is less than or equal to $N-n-1$ (again, refer to Theorem A in Section\tume\ref{prior}).
Then, the following statement is an immediate consequence of Theorem\tume\ref{main}.
\begin{cor}\label{2n<N}
The PEBBLE MOTION PROBLEM ON GENERAL GRAPHS can be solved with time complexity of $\Ord(N^2+n^2\log n)$ 
if $2n < N$ holds.
\end{cor}

But astonishingly, by applying the results from this paper on the board trees, 
even the known time complexity of the reconfiguration algorithm on general $N$-vertex board graphs, 
which is $\Ord(N^3)$, can be truly improved -- except for the special case where $N-n$ is in $\Ord(1)$ 
with respect to $N$, as follows:
%再び、驚くべきことに、本論文においてthe authorsが導いた、board tree上の結果を用いることにより、
%木とは限らない一般のboard graph上のpebble motion problemに対するreconfiguration algorithmに対する
%既知の計算量である$\Ord(N^3)$さえ、改善させることが出来る。
%The PEBBLE MOTION PROBLEM ON TREESにおける入力グラフを一般の$N$頂点グラフに変更した問題を、
%The PEBBLE MOTION PROBLEM ON GENERAL GRAPHSと呼ぶことにする。このとき以下の定理が成り立つ。
\begin{thm}\label{main2}
The PEBBLE MOTION PROBLEM ON GENERAL GRAPHS can be solved with time complexity of $\Ord(N^2+\frac{n^3}{N-n}+n^2\log(\min\{n, N-n\}))$.
\end{thm}

Lastly, we would like to note that by making a few modifications to certain parts of the algorithm 
proposed in this paper, appropriate solutions to the multi-agent path finding problems on trees 
can also be obtained.
%\textcolor{red}{Furthermore, it is also worth noting that the method proposed in this paper can be applied 
%to algorithms on $2$-connected board graphs by using ear decomposition of $2$-connected graphs 
%instead of the decomposition into subtrees of trees in Section $4$.}

