Let us define the puzzle graph ${\rm puz}^{\ast}(X,n)$ of $X$ with $n$ pebbles such that
\begin{equation*}
%  \left\{ 
  \begin{alignedat}{2}   
V({\rm puz}^{\ast}(X,n))&:={\cal F}(X,n), \\
E({\rm puz}^{\ast}(X,n))&:=\{ (f,g) \,:\, f,g \in {\cal F}(X,n), \textrm{$f$ is rearrangeable into $g$ by some moves} \}.
  \end{alignedat} 
%  \right.
\end{equation*}

We say that $(X,n)$ is \textit{transitive} if for any configuration $f \in {\cal F}(X,n)$ and for any vertex $u$ of $X$, 
a pebble $f(u)$ can be shifted to any other vertex by a sequence of finite moves.
For a graph $Z$, let $c(Z)$ be the number of connected components of $Z$.
