\begin{rem}\label{counterexample}
The following statement on page 247, left column, second paragraph from the bottom of Kornhauser, Miller, and Spirakis \cite{715921}:
\begin{quote}
\noindent
{\bf b. G Separable and transitive}

\vspace{5pt}

``If G is a tree, then the proof of transitivity implies that at most $\Ord(n)$ moves are needed to move a pebble anywhere;
so the proof of Theorem 1, Case 2, implies an upper bound of $\Ord(n^2)$ to move all the $k < n$ pebbles.''
\end{quote}
is incorrect. The initial part of the above statement should be corrected as follows:
\begin{quote}
    ``If G is a tree, then the proof of transitivity implies that at most $\Ord(n^2)$ moves are needed
    to move a pebble anywhere;''
\end{quote}
This is because, when pebbles are densely packed in a straight line along a path graph, it may be impossible to relocate
a particular pebble of them unless all pebbles are moved simultaneously.
Consider, for example, a long path with a single pendant edge attached at its center,
and suppose that only the left half of the path is completely filled with pebbles.
In such a case, relocating pebble X, which is initially placed at the leftmost end of the path,
to the rightmost end requires $\Omega(n^2)$ moves.
To move X to the rightmost vertex of the path, X must traverse past $\Omega(n)$ pebbles
that are tightly packed immediately to its right.
Since direct swapping of pebbles is not allowed, X must be temporarily moved to the leaf
at the end of the pendant edge.
However, this requires temporarily relocating all $\Omega(n)$ pebbles that lie to the left of
the attachment vertex of the pendant edge on the path, 
to the portion of the path that lies to the right of that attachment vertex.
This procedure requires $\Omega(n^2)$ moves in total,
because each of the $\Omega(n)$ pebbles must be moved a distance of $\Omega(n)$.
Therefore, a correct revision of their argument should read:
\begin{quote}
\noindent
``If G is a tree, then the proof of transitivity implies that at most $\Ord(n^2)$ moves are needed
to move a pebble anywhere; so the proof of Theorem 1, Case 2, implies an upper bound of
$\Ord(n^3)$ to move all the $k < n$ pebbles.''
\end{quote}
In other words, what they actually proved is an upper bound of $\Ord(n^3)$.
