\begin{Proofof}{Lemma\tume\ref{sbs0}}
We apply the following algorithm.

%\begin{alg}[$\Alg.\Pac(T, \BT, x)$]\hfill
\begin{alg}[$\Alg.sbs0(G, S_1, S_2)$]\hfill
\begin{namelist}{OUTPUT}
\item[\sl Input]{A tree $G$ with $N$ vertices. Subsets of vertices $S_1, S_2  \subset V(G)$ with $|S_1| = |S_2| = n$. }
\item[\sl Output]{A sequence of moves which transfers all the pebbles from $S_1$ to $S_2$.}
\end{namelist}
\begin{alg-enumerate}
\item Make a list $L$ of leaves of $G$. 
\item If $L = \emptyset$, then stop.
Take a leaf $v \in L$.
Let $u$ be a neighbor of $v$.
\item If $v \in S_1 \cap S_2$, then:\\
$\ $ Set $S_1 = S_1 \setminus \{ v \}$, $S_2 = S_2 \setminus \{ v \}$.\\
else if $v \in S_1 \setminus S_2$, then:\\
$\ $ Find a shortest path $\widetilde{P}$ from $v$ to $V(G) \setminus S_1$.\\
$\ $ Let $w$ be another endvertex of $\widetilde{P}$ other than $v$.\\
$\ $ Move pebbles from $V(\widetilde{P}) \setminus \{ w \}$ to $V(\widetilde{P}) \setminus \{ v \}$. \\
$\ $ Set $S_1 = (S_1 \setminus \{ v \}) \cup \{ w \}$.\\
else if $v \in S_2 \setminus S_1$, then:\\
$\ $ Find a shortest path $\widetilde{P}$ from $v$ to $S_1$.\\
$\ $ Let $w$ be another endvertex of $\widetilde{P}$ other than $v$.\\
$\ $ Move a pebble from $w$ to $v$.\\
$\ $ Set $S_1 = S_1 \setminus \{ w \}$, $S_2 = S_2 \setminus \{ v \}$.
\item Set $T = T - v$.
Remove $v$ from $L$.
If $u$ is a leaf of $T$, then add $u$ to $L$.
Goto step \maru{2}. 
\end{alg-enumerate}
\end{alg}

In step \maru{4}, $|V(G)|$ always decreases.
Hence, we have finally $L = \emptyset$.
The running time is dominated by step \maru{3}.
In case $v \in S_1 \cap S_2$ or $v \not\in S_1 \cup S_2$, it takes a constant time.
In case $v \in S_1 \setminus S_2$, it takes $\Ord (n)$ steps to move pebbles along $\widetilde{P}$.
In this case, since $N$ decreases by $1$, by the inductive hypothesis, we get a total running time of $\Ord (n + n(N-1)) \le \Ord (Nn)$.   
In case $v \in S_2 \setminus S_1$, it takes $\Ord (N)$ steps to move pebbles along $\widetilde{P}$.
In this case, since $n$ decreases by $1$, again by the inductive hypothesis, we get a total running time of $\Ord (N + (n-1)N) \le \Ord (Nn)$. 
\end{Proofof} 

