\section{Related Work}
\vspace{-0.5em}

\subsection{Medical Question Answering}
Medical question answering (MedQA) aims to predict accurate answers to domain-specific clinical or biomedical questions, often posed in a multiple-choice format. Existing benchmarks span a variety of formats and reasoning challenges, including clinical exam-style questions, evidence-based inference, and multi-subject distractor-rich scenarios~\cite{pal2022medmcqa}.
%
Recent approaches leverage large language models (LLMs) or chain-of-thought prompting to enhance reasoning~\cite{singhal2025toward}. MedCoT~\cite{liu2024medcot} explicitly integrates multi-step rationale generation with hierarchical expert feedback. Concurrently, biomedical LLMs such as MedLLaMA~\footnote{\url{https://huggingface.co/johnsnowlabs/}}, HuatuoGPT~\cite{chen2024huatuogpt}, and OpenBioLLM~\cite{pal2024openbiollms} have achieved strong zero-shot or few-shot performance on MedQA benchmarks. However, these models typically rely on direct answer generation and lack explicit mechanisms for multi-agent reasoning or consistency verification.
%
In contrast, our method introduces a retrieval-augmented multi-agent framework that performs staged reasoning and employs a dedicated verifier to assess the reliability of generated answers, promoting robustness and interpretability.


\subsection{Retrieval-Augmented Reasoning}

Retrieval-augmented reasoning enhances prediction quality, factual consistency, and interpretability by incorporating external knowledge into the reasoning process. Early works such as RAG~\cite{lewis2020retrieval} retrieve text passages to guide open-domain generation, while later methods extend retrieval to more structured forms, such as few-shot demonstrations~\cite{izacard2023atlas} or intermediate reasoning paths~\cite{shi2023replug}.
%
In the context of chain-of-thought (CoT) prompting, retrieval has been explored to select relevant questions, rationales, or multi-step reasoning exemplars that better align with the target task~\cite{xu2022prompting, he2025inference}. Despite these advances, most existing frameworks rely primarily on question-level similarity and often neglect deeper alignment at the reasoning level.
%
Our framework addresses this limitation by employing a hierarchical retrieval strategy: first retrieving question-option exemplars, then refining based on CoT similarity. This enables alignment in both problem context and reasoning structure, thereby improving downstream multi-agent reasoning.

\subsection{Multi-Agent Systems for Reasoning}
Multi-agent systems (MAS) have emerged as a promising approach to enhance the robustness, diversity, and reliability of reasoning in complex tasks, including medical question answering. By orchestrating multiple reasoning paths or personas, MAS frameworks aim to mitigate biases and capture complementary perspectives, which are particularly critical in high-stakes medical decision-making.
%
Recent studies have explored various MAS paradigms for medical reasoning. MedAgents~\cite{tang2023medagents} proposes a collaborative multi-agent framework where multiple agents independently generate answers and a majority voting scheme determines the final prediction. MDAgents~\cite{kim2024mdagents} further enhances this idea by introducing dynamic collaboration and adaptive feedback mechanisms among agents during the reasoning process. MedPrompt~\cite{chen2024medprompt} adopts a multi-round prompting strategy combined with ensemble voting to improve medical QA performance. Additionally, frameworks like Multi-Persona~\cite{wang2023multipersona} and Self-Refine~\cite{madaan2024selfrefine} leverage self-collaboration and iterative self-feedback to strengthen individual agent reasoning capabilities.
%
While multi-agent collaboration has demonstrated effectiveness in improving answer quality, it also introduces notable challenges. As highlighted in recent evaluations~\cite{tang2023medagents}, excessive agent interactions may lead to reasoning conflicts, unstable decision paths, and increased inference costs. Recent studies~\cite{cemri2025multi} further reveal that over-complex MAS architectures often suffer from systemic failures, such as miscommunication, vague role specification, and weak verification. Moreover, most existing MAS frameworks lack explicit mechanisms to assess the internal consistency between generated reasoning and final answers, which can limit reliability in clinical contexts.
%
To address these limitations, we propose a lightweight General-Specialist Reasoning Collaboration (GSRC) strategy that pairs a generalist and a specialist agent in a complementary manner, promoting stable and robust medical reasoning with minimal inter-agent conflicts.




\section{Methodology}
\label{sec:method}
We propose \textsc{TAGS} (Test-time Generalist–Specialist Reasoning with Retrieval-Augmentation and Uncertainty-Aware Verification), a parameter-efficient framework for medical question answering that operates entirely during inference. At its core is the Generalist–Specialist Reasoning Collaboration (GSRC), a dual-agent design that promotes reasoning diversity and domain alignment without requiring any parameter updates. To support GSRC, we introduce two auxiliary modules: Hierarchical Retrieval Augmentation (HRA), which supplies diverse and rationale-aligned exemplars, and Uncertainty-Aware Answer Aggregation (UAAA), which selects the final answer by evaluating the consistency of each reasoning path. As shown in Figure~\ref{fig_pipeline}, these components form an integrated pipeline that enables robust, zero-shot clinical QA without model fine-tuning.


\subsection{Hierarchical Retrieval Augmentation}
\label{subsec:hra}
Hierarchical Retrieval Augmentation (HRA) grounds reasoning in up-to-date evidence while injecting diverse paths for chain-of-thought (CoT) generation through a two-stage retrieval scheme. We retrieve from a frozen medical-QA corpus $\mathcal{D}$ whose entries are $d_i=(Q_i,O_i,A_i,R_i)$, where $R_i$ denotes the CoT rationale. We use a frozen text encoder $\mathcal{E}(\cdot)$ based on M3-Embedding~\cite{chen2024bge}, with 1024-dimensional output.

\textbf{Stage 1: Initial semantic retrieval.}  
We begin by embedding the query using a frozen encoder. Let $\mathbf{z} = \mathcal{E}(Q \oplus O)$, where the question $Q$ and its options $O$ are concatenated in standard order (A, B, C, D). Cosine similarity is computed against all candidate embeddings $\mathcal{E}(Q_i \oplus O_i)$ in the corpus. The top-$K$ retrieved examples form:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:t1}
\mathcal{T}_1 = \operatorname*{Top\text{-}K} \bigl\{ d_i \in \mathcal{D} : \mathrm{sim}(\mathbf{z}, \mathcal{E}(Q_i \oplus O_i)) \bigr\}.
\end{equation}


\textbf{Stage 2: Rationale-guided retrieval.}  
After Round-1 reasoning yields preliminary rationales $R_{G,1}$ and $R_{S,1}$, we retrieve exemplars whose stored rationales best match these CoTs:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:tg2}
\mathcal{T}_{G,2} = \operatorname*{Top\text{-}K} \bigl\{ d_i \in \mathcal{D} : \mathrm{sim}(\mathbf{r}_G, \mathcal{E}(R_i)) \bigr\},
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:ts2}
\mathcal{T}_{S,2} = \operatorname*{Top\text{-}K} \bigl\{ d_i \in \mathcal{D} : \mathrm{sim}(\mathbf{r}_S, \mathcal{E}(R_i)) \bigr\}.
\end{equation}
By aligning on reasoning paths rather than surface form, Stage 2 injects complementary evidence beyond surface similarity, reducing the limitations of purely semantic matching.


% \subsection{G–S Reasoning Collaboration}
\subsection{Generalist–Specialist Collaboration}
\label{subsec:gsrc}
Given the retrieved exemplar sets from HRA, Generalist–Specialist Reasoning Collaboration (GSRC) performs dual-agent inference in two rounds by coupling broad medical knowledge with focused domain expertise. The system consists of a generalist agent $\mathcal{G}$ and a specialist agent $\mathcal{S}$, both instantiated as prompted roles of the same frozen LLM without parameter updates.

An auxiliary LLM role first infers the medical specialty most relevant to the query $(Q,O)$, yielding a label $s$ (e.g., \texttt{cardiology}). This label is then injected into the prompt for $\mathcal{S}$ as “You are a medical specialist in the field of [s]”, guiding its reasoning toward domain-specific knowledge while preserving the core semantics of $(Q,O)$. Further details are provided in Appendix~\ref{Generalist–Specialist System Roles and Prompt}.
%
The collaboration unfolds in two rounds that iteratively refine rationales and answers.

\textbf{Round 1: Initial hypothesis generation.}  
Both agents receive the query $(Q,O)$ together with the semantically retrieved set
$\mathcal{T}_{1}$ (§\ref{subsec:hra}); the specialist additionally sees the inferred
specialty $s$.  Each agent produces an initial CoT and answer:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:gsrc_r1}
\begin{aligned}
\bigl(R_{G,1},A_{G,1}\bigr) &= \mathcal{G}\!\bigl(Q,O,\mathcal{T}_{1}\bigr),\\
\bigl(R_{S,1},A_{S,1}\bigr) &= \mathcal{S}\!\bigl(Q,O,\mathcal{T}_{1},s\bigr).
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
These preliminary CoTs trigger Stage 2 of HRA, which returns the rationale-aligned
exemplar sets $\mathcal{T}_{G,2}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{S,2}$.

\textbf{Round 2: Refined reasoning with aligned exemplars.}  
Using the tailored sets, the agents generate updated rationales and answers:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:gsrc_r2}
\begin{aligned}
\bigl(R_{G,2},A_{G,2}\bigr) &= \mathcal{G}\!\bigl(Q,O,\mathcal{T}_{G,2}\bigr),\\
\bigl(R_{S,2},A_{S,2}\bigr) &= \mathcal{S}\!\bigl(Q,O,\mathcal{T}_{S,2},s\bigr).
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

\noindent
Finally, the four \emph{(rationale, answer)} pairs are gathered into a candidate set
{\small
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:candidates}
\mathcal{C}
  = \bigl\{\, (R_{k,r},A_{k,r}) \mid k\!\in\!\{G,S\},\; r\!\in\!\{1,2\} \bigr\},
\end{equation}}
which is then forwarded to the Uncertainty-Aware Answer Aggregation module
(§\ref{subsec:uaaa}) for scoring and final selection.


\subsection{Uncertainty-Aware Answer Aggregation}
\label{subsec:uaaa}

Uncertainty-Aware Answer Aggregation (UAAA) takes as input the candidate set $\mathcal{C}$ generated by GSRC (§\ref{subsec:gsrc}) and selects a single high-confidence answer through consistency-based scoring. To accomplish this, we define a \emph{Reasoning Consistency Evaluator} (RCE), implemented as a separate zero-shot role of the same frozen LLM.

Given a candidate pair $(R_k, A_k)$, the RCE assesses how well the rationale supports the answer in the context of the original query $(Q, O)$, and assigns an integer score $c_k \in [0, 5]$, where higher values indicate stronger logical and clinical coherence. The scoring rubric is detailed in Appendix~\ref{A_Reasoning Consistency Evaluator Rubri}.
%
The final answer is selected as:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:uaaa_select}
A_{\text{final}} = A_{k^\star},
\quad
k^\star = \underset{k \in \mathcal{C}}{\arg\max}\; c_k.
\end{equation}

In the case of ties, preference is resolved deterministically in the following order: specialist round 2, generalist round 2, specialist round 1, and generalist round 1. By explicitly verifying the internal consistency of each reasoning path, UAAA mitigates hallucination propagation and stabilizes final predictions, all without any parameter updates.


