\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setting}
\textbf{Retrieval Dataset.}
We use the MedReason dataset~\cite{wu2025medreason} as our external retrieval corpus. It contains 32,682 medical QA pairs with clinically validated, step-by-step explanations generated via a knowledge graph–guided pipeline based on PrimeKG~\cite{chandak2023building}. Unlike general CoT datasets, MedReason ensures factual correctness by filtering out chains that do not lead to the correct answer. We treat it as a structured knowledge base for retrieving semantically or logically relevant examples at inference. Dataset construction details and examples are provided in Appendix~\ref{app_refenrence_medreason}.



\textbf{Test Datasets.} 
We evaluate TAGS on a curated benchmark of nine medical QA datasets selected from the MEDAGENTSBENCH framework~\cite{tang2025medagentsbench}, designed to assess complex clinical reasoning. The benchmark includes challenging subsets from:
\textbf{MedQA}~\cite{jin2021medqa}, a multilingual board-exam dataset (e.g., USMLE);
\textbf{PubMedQA}~\cite{jin2019pubmedqa}, derived from biomedical literature with yes/no/maybe answers;
\textbf{MedMCQA}~\cite{pal2022medmcqa}, covering 21 medical subjects from Indian medical exams;
\textbf{MedBullets}~\cite{chen2024medbullet}, featuring long-context clinical questions;
\textbf{MedExQA}~\cite{kim2024medexqa}, emphasizing explainable QA across five specialties;
\textbf{MedXpertQA}~\cite{zuo2025medxpertqa}, with subsets targeting reasoning and understanding;
\textbf{MMLU}~\cite{hendrycks2020mmlu} and \textbf{MMLU-Pro} \cite{wang2024mmlu}, general benchmarks with medical subfields.

To better reflect real-world difficulty, we follow the hard subset construction pipeline proposed by MEDAGENTSBENCH. Questions are selected based on model failure rates (<50\% accuracy across a set of strong models), medical topic coverage, and reasoning depth. Specifically, we include 100 hard questions each from MedQA, PubMedQA, MedMCQA, MedExQA, and MMLU-Pro; 100 from each MedXpertQA subset (Reasoning and Understanding); 89 from MedBullets; and 73 from MMLU. This results in a total of 862 expert-verified instances designed to stress-test the reasoning capabilities of large language models.

