        Figure \ref{fig:partii-questionnaire-results} presents participants' responses to the baseline question (\textbf{B}) about their original understanding of presbyopia and the six questions (Q1-Q6) assessing various aspects of the simulation experience. Participants initially reported a low understanding of presbyopia (B: $M = 2.37$, $median = 2$), with responses concentrated between ``No knowledge'' and the midpoint of the scale.

        The simulation experience yielded consistently positive responses across all measures: (1) \underline{Noticing Differences}  (Q1): Participants strongly agreed that they noticed clear differences in their ability to focus on near objects ($M = 5.84$, $median = 6$). (2) \underline{Increased Confidence} (Q2): After the simulation, participants reported significantly higher confidence in their understanding of presbyopia ($M = 5.95$, $median = 6$). (3) \underline{Empathy Development} (Q3): The simulation was highly effective in fostering empathy ($M = 6.37$, $median = 6$), with responses clustered at the upper end of the scale. (4) \underline{Imagining Daily Impact} (Q4): Participants found it easy to imagine how presbyopia might affect daily activities ($M = 6.47$, $median = 7$), showing the highest mean score among all questions. (5) \underline{Recommendation Likelihood} (Q5): There was a strong inclination to recommend the simulation experience to others ($M = 6.16$, $median = 7$), although with slightly more variability in responses. (6) \underline{Influence on Design Decisions} (Q6): For the subset of participants involved in design related domains (n = 11), the experience showed potential to influence future design decisions ($M = 5.85$, $median = 5$), although with a greater spread of responses compared to other questions.

        The visualization reveals a clear shift from the low baseline understanding to consistently high scores across all aspects of the simulation experience. Notably, questions related to empathy (Q3) and imagining daily impacts (Q4) received the highest and most consistent ratings. To assess the overall effectiveness of the simulation, we calculated a composite score across all six questions for each participant. The mean composite score was 6.11 ($SD = 0.68$, $median = 6.17$), indicating a strongly positive overall response to the simulation experience. Regarding the statistical analysis, we conducted a Wilcoxon signed-rank test to compare the baseline understanding (Q0) with the post-simulation confidence in understanding presbyopia (Q2). The test revealed a statistically significant increase ($Z = -3.84$, $p < .001$), with a large effect size ($r = 0.62$).

        To explore potential relationships between variables, we performed Spearman's rank correlation analyses. Notable correlations included: A moderate positive correlation between age and baseline understanding ($r_s = 0.43$, $p < .05$). A strong positive correlation between empathy development (Q3) and imagining daily impact (Q4) ($r_s = 0.72$, $p < .001$). A moderate positive correlation between noticing differences in near focus (Q1) and increased understanding confidence (Q2) ($r_s = 0.51$, $p < .05$). Investigating the potential relationship between participants' vision types and their perception of the presbyopia simulation, we analyzed the relationship between participants' eyesight and their responses to questions Q1 through Q6, which assessed various aspects of the presbyopia simulation experience. Participants were categorized based on their reported vision type: normal vision (3, 15.8\%), myopia (12, 63.2\%), and post-LASIK (4, 21.1\%). Table \ref{tab:eyesight-responses} presents the mean scores and standard deviations for each question across the three eyesight categories.

