	\section{Experimental Results}

	We evaluate the performance of {\fontfamily{lmtt}\selectfont QMLHFL} on two benchmark datasets: MNIST and CIFAR-10. Experiments are conducted under 3-layer and 4-layer hierarchical setups, with a 2-layer setup serving as a benchmark for traditional hierarchical FL.\footnote{Due to the limited computational power of our simulation system and the theoretical nature of this work, we were unable to provide experiments with setups involving more layers.} The performance is measured in terms of test accuracy, i.e., the classification accuracy on a held-out test set, and training loss at each global iteration
	$t$.
	\subsection{Data Distribution and Hierarchical Setups}
	To simulate real-world data heterogeneity, each dataset is partitioned across devices, with each device receiving 1000 local training samples drawn according to one of three scenarios: from 2 randomly selected classes (Case 1), from 6 randomly selected classes (Case 2), or uniformly from all 10 classes (Case 3).

	We consider a hierarchical architecture with 40 devices at the device layer ($\cal C$), 8 edge servers at the first edge layer (${\cal C}_1$), 4 edge servers at the second edge layer (${\cal C}_2$), 2 edge servers at the third edge layer (${\cal C}_3$), and a cloud server. The number of quantization levels at these layers are set as $s_1 = 4$, $s_2 = 8$, $s_3 = 10$, and $s_4 = 12$, respectively.\footnote{In our work, the quantization error variance $q$ is measured numerically.} In the 4-layer setup, each group of 5 devices is connected to one server in ${\cal C}_1$, every 2 servers in ${\cal C}_1$ are connected to one server in ${\cal C}_2$, and every 2 servers in ${\cal C}_2$ are connected to one server in ${\cal C}_3$. For the 3-layer setup, the ${\cal C}_1$ layer is removed, and each group of 10 devices is directly connected to one server in ${\cal C}_2$, with two ${\cal C}_2$ servers connected to one ${\cal C}_3$ server. For the 2-layer setup, both ${\cal C}_1$ and ${\cal C}_2$ layers are removed, and each group of 20 devices is directly connected to one server in ${\cal C}_3$. Removing the first edge layer (${\cal C}_1$) results in a 10-fold increase in the distance between devices and their assigned servers, while removing both ${\cal C}_1$ and ${\cal C}_2$ leads to a 25-fold increase in the distance compared to the original 4-layer setup, which has the reference channel gain $h$ as given in Table~I.

