%\subsubsection{The Leaky Integrate-and-Fire Model} 
\textbf{The Leaky Integrate-and-Fire Model: }
% Add that J and i are considered constant for the ODE's over dt
A model of neuronal dynamics that we seek to study is the leaky integrator, also known as the leaky integrate-and-fire \cite{burkitt2006review,naud2012performance} (LIF) neuron. Historically, the equations that characteristically described the LIF were first proposed by Stein \cite{stein1965theoretical} in $1965$. Since their conception, the dynamics for an LIF's voltage ($v^\ell_k$) have been distilled to the following ordinary differential equation (ODE): 
\begin{align}
    \tau_v \frac{\partial v^\ell_k(t)}{\partial t} = -\gamma_v \big(v^\ell_k(t) - v_{\text{rest}}) + R^\ell j^\ell_k(t) \label{eqn:lif_voltage}
\end{align} %% tau, gamma, R
where $\tau_v$ is the membrane potential time constant, $\gamma_v$ is the voltage leak coefficient, $v_{\text{rest}}$ is the membrane resting potential, and $R^\ell$ is the membrane resistance. Note that $j(t)$ is considered constant for the ODE above over $\partial t$.

The current that enters the LIF cell, $j^\ell_k(t)$, also evolves according to its own dynamics as dictated by the ODE below:
\begin{align}
    \tau_j \frac{\partial j^\ell_k(t)}{\partial t} = -\gamma_j j^\ell_k(t) + \kappa^\ell i^\ell_k(t)
\end{align}
where $\gamma_j$ is the electrical current leak coefficient. Note that $i(t)$ is considered constant for the ODE above over $\partial t$. $i^\ell_k(t)$ itself could be directly-injected electrical current or the sum of weight values for non-zero pre-synaptic emission as follows:
\begin{align}
    i^\ell_k(t) = \sum_i
    \begin{cases}
        W^\ell_{ik} & s^{\ell-1}_i = 1\\
        0 & \text{otherwise}.
    \end{cases} 
\end{align}
where $s^{\ell-1}_i$ is a binary value denoting if there is a spike emission from pre-synaptic neuron $i$. The equation governing the spike emissions ($s^\ell_k)$ for a given LIF neuron $k$ is as follows
\begin{align}
    s^\ell_k(t) = 
    \begin{cases}
        1 & v^\ell_k(t) > \theta^\ell_k(t) \\
        0 & \text{otherwise}
    \end{cases} \label{eqn:spike_emission}
\end{align}
where $\theta^\ell_k(t)$ is the membrane voltage threshold (which, once breached, results in the neuron emitting a discrete spike pulse). Furthermore, once a neuron $k$ has emitted a pulse, it is hyper-polarized to a reset target voltage value $v_{\text{reset}}$:
\begin{align}
    v^\ell_k(t + \Delta t) \leftarrow v^\ell_k(t + \Delta t) \big(1 - s^\ell_k(t)\big) + v_{\text{reset}} s^\ell_k(t).
\end{align}
The voltage threshold is further made to be a function of time $t$ and constructed to adhere to its own dynamics; this results in an `adaptive threshold'. Concretely, the threshold is a combination of a time-varying homeostatic (shift) variable $\hat{\theta}^\ell_k(t)$ and a fixed base threshold value $\theta^\ell_{\text{base}}$ yielding:
\begin{align}
    \frac{\partial \hat{\theta}^\ell_k(t)}{\partial t} = -\frac{1}{\tau_\theta}\hat{\theta}^\ell_k(t) + \kappa_\theta s^\ell_k(t), \quad \theta^\ell_k(t) = \theta^\ell_{\text{base}} + \hat{\theta}^\ell_k(t)
\end{align}
where $\tau_\theta$ is the homeostatic variable time constant and $\kappa_\theta$ is the variable increment value. 
In Figure \ref{fig:latency_lif_dynamics}, we perform a small experiment examining the resulting dynamics of an LIF cell that is driven by a triplet of input latency-coding cells. 

