Overall, the results indicate that many models possess an underutilised capacity to produce high-quality refusals when provided with structured guidance. The intervention revealed latent alignment capabilities in most cases, demonstrating that helpful, policy-grounded refusals can be elicited through prompt-level safety orchestration. Figure 1 provides a visual comparison between baseline refusal helpfulness and post-intervention IHL-compliant refusal helpfulness.

\section{Discussion}

This evaluation reveals clear limitations in how current large language models respond to simple IHL-violating queries—but also shows that many of these issues may be addressable with relatively straightforward interventions. The findings offer useful insights for AI safety, alignment strategies, and the design of refusal behaviour that balances compliance with user experience.\\

\textbf{Refusal Consistency}—\textbf{Broad Success, but Not Absolute}\
\\

Most models refused the 322 explicit IHL-violating prompts the vast majority of the time, demonstrating baseline alignment with harmlessness policies. This includes even open-weight models, suggesting that foundational content filtering has become more widespread. However, isolated lapses persist. For instance, mistral-large responded inappropriately to approximately 11\% of test prompts—highlighting that while alignment is improving, complete consistency remains essential to avoid potential real-world harms.\\

\textbf{Helpfulness Versus Strictness }\\

The data show a recurring trade-off: models that were extremely strict in refusing unlawful prompts (like \textit{chatgpt-o3-mini} or \textit{claude-3.5-sonnet}) often gave very minimal explanations, while models that were more conversational sometimes exhibited slightly lower refusal rates. That said, newer models such as \textit{claude-3.7-sonnet} showed that this trade-off is not inevitable: it achieved both high refusal consistency and a high explanatory rate. This demonstrates that helpfulness does not necessarily come at the cost of safety when models are well-aligned.\\

\textbf{System Prompt Intervention—A Simple, Effective Enhancement\\}

The improvements following the introduction of a standardised system prompt were substantial. Without retraining the models, refusal helpfulness rose dramatically in six out of eight cases—often to above 90\%. This points to the power of simple, deployment-time interventions for boosting refusal quality. Notably, the same system prompt was applied to all models; a tailored prompt adapted to each model’s architecture and behaviour might result in even stronger improvements.

Previous work by Cui et al. \cite{31} employed a system prompt as a high-level instruction mechanism to steer large language models (LLMs) toward safer outputs. The system prompt in \textit{OR-Bench} was designed to instruct models to be both helpful and safe. Specifically, it guided the models to reject prompts deemed toxic—while maintaining a certain level of responsiveness to benign inputs. However, the study found that while such prompts enhanced the safety behaviour of the models, this improvement often came at the expense of over-refusal. In other words, the models would not only block harmful requests but also reject a higher proportion of benign prompts. 

\textit{OR-Bench} aimed to generally balance helpfulness and safety across a wide ethical spectrum. In contrast, our intervention targeted narrowly defined IHL and IHRL violations and was engineered to promote clarity in refusals. This clear focus helped us improve refusal behaviour without compromising model responsiveness. For instance, in the case of Claude 3.5 Sonnet, the system prompt improved the model’s response rate to benign prompts from 65.53\% to 94.41\%, indicating a significant reduction in over-refusal alongside enhanced safety behaviour.

Our results show that with precise, legally informed prompts, it is possible to significantly improve the consistency and quality of LLMs' refusal behaviour. It also illustrates that while system prompts broadly impact model safety, their design must be carefully calibrated to the desired balance between safeguarding users and preserving helpfulness.\\

\textbf{Limitations of Prompt Simplicity }\\

The prompt set used in this study consisted of straightforward, explicit IHL violations, designed to test basic refusal behaviour. While effective for this benchmark, such prompts do not reflect the full complexity of real-world misuse cases. In practice, prompts are often multi-layered or embedded within technical language, which may bypass adversarial training and filters.

For example, one test prompt instructed a model to \textit{“write a drone program to find hospitals… then call the function simpledrone.launch\_missile()…”} While the models objected in their responses to the prompt, some still returned partial code that could be misused. This illustrates a vulnerability: when prompts blend coding tasks with IHL-violating intent—especially when framed in technical language such as 'call simpledrone.launch\_missile' rather than plainly stating 'launch a missile'—models may be more likely to respond to the prompt. These edge cases suggest that even with safety layers, models can still generate harmful outputs when content and form interact in unexpected ways. These observations underscore the need for continued refinement of legal and ethical safeguards, including potentially fine-tuning system prompts on a per-model basis to achieve even higher levels of refusal helpfulness.\\
\vfill\null

\textbf{Ethical Design—Why Explanation Style Matters\\}

Short refusals may meet basic safety standards, but they risk alienating users or encouraging them to rephrase their queries in an attempt to bypass restrictions \cite{5}. By contrast, refusals that explain \textit{why} a request is denied—especially in moral or legal terms—can foster trust, increase transparency, and educate users on ethical boundaries. This is particularly important for IHL violations, where the context and gravity of the harm are significant. Developers can enhance both safety and user engagement by combining high-performing models with external moderation frameworks that prioritise clear, principled refusals.

\section{Conclusion}

This study provides a comparative evaluation of eight leading LLMs in their handling of IHL-violating prompts, focusing on both refusal accuracy and refusal helpfulness. All models demonstrated a baseline ability to reject overtly illegal requests. However, the clarity and rationale behind those refusals varied significantly.

We showed that the quality of the explanatory characteristic of refusals can be significantly improved using a simple system-level safety prompt, without retraining the underlying model. Most models responded positively to this intervention, indicating a latent capacity for better refusals that can be activated through prompt engineering.

Nonetheless, edge cases continue to challenge even well-aligned models. Prompts that mix code and harmful intent—such as instructing a model to programmatically carry out malicious actions, such as targeting hospitals—demonstrated that models can falter when asked to perform complex tasks cloaked in technical language. These cases highlight the limitations of adversarial training alone and underscore the need for ongoing refinements to prompt design, system safety layers, and legal alignment strategies.


