\subsection{Performance Comparisons}
\noindent
At first, we check the performance gap between \ac{fl} and a \ac{csgd}, assuming a \emph{Genie} knows all training datasets from all users, method. 
Since the Genie has access to all datasets, we consider its performance to indicate the upper bound of \ac{ml} methods.  
As such, when we have privacy concerns and are required to use \ac{fl} to train our model, we expect the globally trained model to suffer from statistical data heterogeneity.
Our results in Table \ref{tab:fl_vs_sgd} also validate this: \ac{fedavg} delivers $99.4\%$ of the \ac{csgd}'s test accuracy. 


We then investigate how the prediction accuracy behaves across different mini-slots in our video caching task. 
As the content request models follow a popularity-preference tradeoff, predicting the exact requests in all future mini-slots can be challenging. 
Our simulation results show that the accuracies are $0.8531 \pm 0.0045$, $0.8167 \pm 0.0046$, $0.7968 \pm 0.0051$, $0.7771 \pm 0.0061$, and $0.7414 \pm 0.0047$, in mini-slots $1$ to $5$, respectively, yielding an average test accuracy of $0.7970 \pm 0.0049$. 
% These results also bolster our intuition of estimating the actual content requests based on Proposition \ref{prop}, which does not entirely rely on the prediction results from the Transformer.
Given these facts and results, we now focus on performance comparisons with other baselines.


