\paragraph{\textbf{Example: Nash equilibrium}}
In game theory, a \textit{Nash equilibrium} describes a situation where no
player can improve their outcome by unilaterally changing their strategy,
assuming all other players maintain their current strategies.


A classic example is the \textit{prisoner's dilemma}, illustrated in the table
below. Here, the loss is 
represented
as a pair, indicating the respective
prison sentences for prisoner A and prisoner B.
If both
