(regular contexts do not handle operations), and $x\type \tau \vdash F[x]\type \sigma\etype \epsilon_1$.
%
We therefore have  $\lambda^{\epsilon_1} x\type \tau.\, (\then{\epsilon_1}{F[x]}{\myg})\type \tau \to \real!\epsilon_1$, as
$\myg\type \sigma \to \real\etype\epsilon_2$, with $\epsilon_2 \subseteq \epsilon_1$.



So, by the induction hypothesis, for some $r$ and $e_1'\type \epsilon_1$  we have $\lambda^{\epsilon_1} x\type \tau.\, (\then{\epsilon_1}{F[x]}{\myg}) \vdash_{\epsilon_1} e_1 \xrightarrow{r} e_1'$, and $e_1'\type \tau\etype\epsilon_1$.
%
So by the rule for regular contexts, we have $\myg \vdash_{\epsilon_1} F[e_1]\xrightarrow{r} F[e_1]$, 
and, by Lemma~\ref{Alem:ctx} we also have $F[e_1']\type \sigma\etype\epsilon_1$.


\item Suppose $e$ has the form $S[e_1]$, with $e_1$ non-terminal where $S = \wph{h}{v}{\square}$.  
