$h\type \parr,\sigma_1\etype\epsilon_1\ell \Rightarrow \sigma\etype \epsilon_1$,
$v\type\parr$, and 
$e_1\type \sigma_1\etype \epsilon_1\ell$, 
for some $\parr$, $\sigma_1$, and $\ell$.
%
So for $\return \mapsto e_r \in h$, we have 
$e_r\type (\parr,\sigma_1) \to \sigma\etype \epsilon_1$.
%
So we have 
$\lambda^{\epsilon_1} x\type \sigma.\, (\then{\epsilon}{v_r(v,x)}{\myg}\type \sigma \to \real\etype \epsilon_1$.

Then, by the induction hypothesis we have 
$\lambda^{\epsilon_1} x\type \sigma.\, (\then{\epsilon_1}{v_r(v,x)}{\myg}) \vdash_{\epsilon_1\ell} e_1 \evalto{r} e_1'$ for some $r$ and 
$e_1'\type \sigma_1\etype \epsilon_1\ell$.
%
So by the context rule for $S$ we have $\myg \vdash_\epsilon \wph{h}{v}{e} \evalto{r} \wph{h}{v}{e'}$, and we see that $\wph{h}{v}{e'}\type \sigma\etype\epsilon_1$, as required.







\item Suppose $e$ has the form $S[e_1]$, with $e_1$ non-terminal where $S = \then{\epsilon}{\square}{(\lambda^{\epsilon} x\type \tau.e_2)}$.
